      "Costs more than a standard window unit of the same BTU",
      "Casement installs need a filler panel and more work",
      "Fixed-speed compressor"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If your windows slide sideways or crank open, this is the right answer before you settle for a portable AC.",
    "description": "Standard window air conditioners are built for double-hung windows that open vertically. If yours slide side to side or crank outward, a normal box unit will not fit, and many people give up and buy a portable AC. The Frigidaire FFRS0822SE is the better alternative: a tall, narrow 8,000 BTU room air conditioner designed specifically for slider and casement windows. It uses electronic controls with a remote, a washable filter and a standard 115-volt plug. Because the whole unit sits in the window with the hot side outside, it avoids the efficiency penalty of a portable AC that pulls conditioned air out of the room through its exhaust. Casement installs still take more work, usually a filler panel cut to fit, and the price is higher than a double-hung unit of similar capacity. For a narrow window AC that actually fits a slider, it is one of the few widely available choices."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Small Window Air Conditioner",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-small-window-air-conditioner/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size window AC do I need for a small bedroom?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Energy Star suggests 5,000 BTU for 100 to 150 square feet and 6,000 BTU for 150 to 250. Add about 10 percent for a very sunny room and reduce it for a shaded one. Most small bedrooms need 5,000 BTU, but a top-floor room or one with afternoon sun often does better with 6,000 BTU."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best narrow window AC?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For horizontal slider or casement windows, the Frigidaire FFRS0822SE is built specifically for that tall, narrow opening and is widely available. A standard box unit will not fit a slider. If no window unit will fit at all, a portable AC is the fallback, but it is noticeably less efficient than a window unit of the same rating."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is an inverter window AC worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a bedroom you sleep in all summer, yes. An inverter compressor slows down instead of cycling on and off, so it is quieter, holds a steadier temperature, removes more humidity and uses less energy. For a guest room, garage or dorm used occasionally, a basic fixed-speed 5,000 BTU unit is the better value."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I run a small window AC on a generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. A 5,000 or 6,000 BTU unit is an easy load for a 2,000-watt-class inverter generator. Run the generator outdoors only, away from windows, doors and vents, with CO alarms inside. Connect the AC with an outdoor-rated extension cord and never backfeed the house; a licensed electrician should install a transfer switch for anything more."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long will a power station run a window AC?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not long. A 5,000 BTU unit draws roughly 400 to 500 watts while the compressor runs, so a 1,000 watt-hour station lasts on the order of a couple of hours, less in the heat. The station also needs enough surge capacity for the compressor start. Inverter units help, but overnight cooling needs a much larger battery system or a generator."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I put a window AC in a camper?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many owners fit a 5,000 BTU unit in a pop-up, teardrop or trailer conversion, but treat it as a parked-only setup. Household units are not built for towing vibration, so remove or properly support them before travel. Build a weather-tight frame, let it drain outside, and run it on shore power or a generator kept well away from the camper."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it safe to buy a window AC on Amazon?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, from the brand or a reputable seller. Inspect the unit on arrival for dents, bent fins and oil stains, and keep the packaging until it has run for a few days. If it may have shipped on its side, let it stand upright for the time the manual recommends before first use so the compressor oil can settle."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
