          The best small window air conditioner is the one sized to the room, shaped for the window, and quiet enough to sleep beside. Get those three right and even a budget unit is a good buy; get one wrong and no feature list will save it.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. BTU per Square Foot: Sizing a Small Window AC
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common mistake with a small window AC is buying by price and hoping it is big enough. Energy Star's sizing guidance is the right starting point: about 5,000 BTU for 100 to 150 square feet, 6,000 BTU for 150 to 250, 7,000 BTU for 250 to 300, and 8,000 BTU for 300 to 350 square feet. Measure the room's length and width, multiply, and start there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then adjust. Energy Star suggests adding about 10 percent for a very sunny room and reducing by about 10 percent for a heavily shaded one. Add roughly 600 BTU for each person beyond two who regularly uses the room, and add around 4,000 BTU if the unit is cooling a kitchen. A top-floor bedroom under an uninsulated roof, or a home office full of computers, behaves like a bigger room than its floor area suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oversizing is not free insurance. A unit that is too big cools the air quickly and shuts off before it has run long enough to pull moisture out, leaving the room cold and clammy. That matters in humid climates, where dehumidification is half of what makes a room comfortable. If damp air is the real problem, especially below grade, a dedicated dehumidifier from our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">best basement dehumidifier</a> guide may be the better buy. Inverter units are more forgiving of oversizing because they slow down instead of shutting off.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most small bedrooms, a 5,000 BTU unit is enough. If the room is between 150 and 250 square feet, or gets afternoon sun, a 6,000 BTU unit like the LG LW6017R is the safer choice. Above that, the 8,000 BTU Midea U-shaped inverter covers rooms up to about 350 square feet, and it does so more quietly than any fixed-speed unit.
          </p>

