        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size window AC do I need for a small bedroom?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Energy Star suggests 5,000 BTU for 100 to 150 square feet and 6,000 BTU for 150 to 250. Add about 10 percent for a very sunny room and reduce it for a shaded one. Most small bedrooms need 5,000 BTU, but a top-floor room or one with afternoon sun often does better with 6,000 BTU.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best narrow window AC?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For horizontal slider or casement windows, the Frigidaire FFRS0822SE is built specifically for that tall, narrow opening and is widely available. A standard box unit will not fit a slider. If no window unit will fit at all, a portable AC is the fallback, but it is noticeably less efficient than a window unit of the same rating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an inverter window AC worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a bedroom you sleep in all summer, yes. An inverter compressor slows down instead of cycling on and off, so it is quieter, holds a steadier temperature, removes more humidity and uses less energy. For a guest room, garage or dorm used occasionally, a basic fixed-speed 5,000 BTU unit is the better value.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a small window AC on a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A 5,000 or 6,000 BTU unit is an easy load for a 2,000-watt-class inverter generator. Run the generator outdoors only, away from windows, doors and vents, with CO alarms inside. Connect the AC with an outdoor-rated extension cord and never backfeed the house; a licensed electrician should install a transfer switch for anything more.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a power station run a window AC?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not long. A 5,000 BTU unit draws roughly 400 to 500 watts while the compressor runs, so a 1,000 watt-hour station lasts on the order of a couple of hours, less in the heat. The station also needs enough surge capacity for the compressor start. Inverter units help, but overnight cooling needs a much larger battery system or a generator.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put a window AC in a camper?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many owners fit a 5,000 BTU unit in a pop-up, teardrop or trailer conversion, but treat it as a parked-only setup. Household units are not built for towing vibration, so remove or properly support them before travel. Build a weather-tight frame, let it drain outside, and run it on shore power or a generator kept well away from the camper.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to buy a window AC on Amazon?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, from the brand or a reputable seller. Inspect the unit on arrival for dents, bent fins and oil stains, and keep the packaging until it has run for a few days. If it may have shipped on its side, let it stand upright for the time the manual recommends before first use so the compressor oil can settle.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
